### Iterative Version:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int customSequence(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;      // First number
    if (n == 1) return -1;     // Second number
    if (n == 2) return -1;     // Third number

    // Iterative calculation
    int a = 0, b = -1, c = -1, result = 0;
    for (int i = 3; i <= n; i++) {
        result = a + b + c; // Sum of the three preceding numbers
        a = b;
        b = c;
        c = result;
    }
    return result;
}

int main() {
    int n;

    // Input: Get the position of the number in the sequence
    cout << "Enter the position of the number in the sequence (n): ";
    cin >> n;

    if (n < 0) {
        cout << "Invalid input! Please enter a non-negative integer." << endl;
    } else {
        // Output: Display the nth number in the sequence
        cout << "The " << n << "th number in the sequence is: " << customSequence(n) << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This iterative version avoids the overhead of recursion and is more efficient for larger values of `n`.

Here's a simple HTML website that displays a random joke from a list of four jokes when the user clicks a button. The functionality is implemented using JavaScript:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Random Joke Generator</title>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      text-align: center;
      margin: 50px;
    }
    button {
      padding: 10px 20px;
      font-size: 16px;
      cursor: pointer;
      background-color: #007BFF;
      color: white;
      border: none;
      border-radius: 5px;
    }
    button:hover {
      background-color: #0056b3;
    }
    #joke {
      margin-top: 20px;
      font-size: 18px;
      color: #333;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Random Joke Generator</h1>
  <button onclick="showRandomJoke()">Tell me a joke!</button>
  <p id="joke"></p>

  <script>
    // List of jokes
    const jokes = [
      "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!",
      "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
      "Why don't skeletons fight each other? They don't have the guts.",
      "What do you call fake spaghetti? An impasta!"
    ];

    // Function to show a random joke
    function showRandomJoke() {
      const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
      const randomJoke = jokes[randomIndex];
      document.getElementById('joke').textContent = randomJoke;
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works:
1. The `jokes` array contains four jokes.
2. When the user clicks the "Tell me a joke!" button, the `showRandomJoke` function is called.
3. The function generates a random index using `Math.random()` and selects a joke from the `jokes` array.
4. The selected joke is displayed in the `<p>` element with the ID `joke`.

You can copy and paste this code into an `.html` file and open it in a web browser to see it in action!

